🤔 Thinking: Architecture decision, to record and follow:

1. `crates/core` — pure Rust: args_parser, ignore_files, require_package, 
cli_options (option table from bin/jscodeshift.js), discovery 
(getAllFiles/dirFiles), runner (orchestration, logging, stats, transform 
download to temp).
2. `crates/cli` — binary `jscodeshift`, replicating bin/jscodeshift.js: parse 
args, stdin mode, error/exit behavior.
3. `crates/node` — napi addon exposing the core API 
(core/Collection/collections/matchNode/template/getParser) to JS transforms, 
plus `runWorker` implementing Worker.js file loop (read, call transform, print, 
write-file-atomic, status protocol).
4. JS bridge kept minimal: `bin/jscodeshift.js` launcher -> Rust binary; 
`index.js` -> addon; `bridge/transform-host.js` — sets up @babel/register, 
requires the transform module, hands it to the Rust addon worker loop.

Milestone order (each verified against the baseline before moving on):
M1: core args_parser + cli_options + require_package + CLI entry => exact 
`--help`, `--version`, no-path error, exit codes.
M2: ignore_files + discovery => file selection parity.
M3: runner + worker + addon core API => full transform parity via upstream jest 
suite.

Start with M1 now.

